"""Real geometry for the door-side cart on the FF1763940475070 INSIDE clip.

Every number here was read out of that run's own tracking JSON
(FF1763940475070_E827250FC2BA-medium-INSIDE_tracking.json), so it is what the
pipeline actually saw. 1280x720, 20 fps, 417 frames.

WHAT THE CLIP SHOWS
-------------------
C2 is a cart standing at the door, upper-right of frame, loaded and unbagged. P3
is the person who is with it: the linker gives them the cart at frame 28 and they
hold it, in contact, until frame 124 — IoU peak 0.363, mean 0.132 over frames
20-164. Then they step away from it, still in plain view, and at frame 164 they
leave the frame entirely. The cart is still there, still loaded and unbagged, and
at frames 338-343 it rolls out of the store at 75.

They never PUSH it while linked: over the frames they are in contact, 28-124, the
cart's centroid sweeps 12.2 px against a LINK_STATIC_SPREAD_PX bar of 12.0, and
over the window the replay below actually links on, 11.7 px. So `_is_parked` is
true when the drift counter matures, the no-taker parked branch of Step 0.5
releases the link, and — since nobody else ever touches this cart — the takeover
branch never fires.

WHY THAT MATTERED
-----------------
The release used to report the cart DISOWNED, which makes the tracker forget the
remembered owner. P3 then walked out 34 frames later with nobody on record as the
cart's owner, `abandoned` stayed False for all 412 frames of the track, and the
run finalised 75 HIGH PRIORITY instead of PUSHOUT ALERT — the score never
changed, only the flag that `classify_event()` needs at HIGH_SCORE.

The 1764200318790 shopper this branch was written for is the case it must still
catch: peak IoU 0.064 over six frames of corner overlap. See LINK_OWNED_PEAK_IOU
and tests/fixtures/parked_cart_geometry.py.

REPLAYING IT
------------
The slice starts at frame 20, eight frames before the run's own link, so the
candidate accumulator starts later here and the link forms at frame 40 instead of
28. Everything the release depends on — contact, its end, the cart standing
still, the owner leaving — is inside the slice.

Boxes are (x1, y1, x2, y2) and centroids (cx, cy), exactly as the linker receives
them.
"""

